---
import {Image} from "astro:assets";
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import "../../styles/uchicago.scss";
import uChicago from "../../assets/images/project-notes/chicago.png";
import logo from "../../assets/images/project-notes/uchicago-logo-mybg.png";
import bg from "../../assets/images/uchicago-bg.svg";
---

<BaseLayout title="Making accessibility findings actionable">
    <main class="container case-study case-study--uchicago">
        <article class="case-study project-note">
            <div class="hero hero--project hero--project--uchicago">
                <p class="hero--project__client">
                    <span class="sr-only">University of Chicago</span>
                    <Image src={logo} format="webp"
                           alt="Imperial"
                           class="client-logo  imperial-logo"/>
                </p>

                <h1 class="hero--project__description easy-read">
                    Making accessibility findings actionable
                </h1>

                <p class="hero--project__summary easy-read">
                    After adapting the UChicago Knowledge repository to the University of Chicago’s visual
                    identity, I evaluated the interface for accessibility. I assessed the severity of the
                    issues I found and translated them into practical recommendations the client and
                    development team could act upon.
                </p>

                <p class="tag">
                    <span>Accessibility evaluation</span>
                </p>
            </div>

            <section class="case-study__section">
                <div class="easy-read">
                    <h2>Working within a shared platform</h2>

                    <p>
                        UChicago Knowledge is an institutional repository built on InvenioRDM, an open-source
                        platform used by research organisations around the world. Its core structure and
                        interactions were already established, so the initial design work focused on adapting
                        the interface to feel recognisably part of the University of Chicago.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I implemented the visual changes directly in the front end, applying the university’s
                        typography, colours and branding while retaining the familiar InvenioRDM interaction
                        patterns. Working closely with the implementation also gave me a detailed understanding
                        of the repository’s components and recurring interface patterns.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <figure class="case-study-figure case-study-figure--uchicago-homepage">
                    <Image src={uChicago} format="webp"
                           alt=""/>
                    <figcaption class="board-note">
                        I adapted the InvenioRDM interface to the University of Chicago’s visual identity while
                        preserving the platform’s established structure and interactions.
                    </figcaption>
                </figure>
            </section>

            <section class="case-study__section easy-read">
                    <h2>Evaluating impact, not just conformance</h2>

                    <p>
                        I then carried out a structured accessibility evaluation of the repository. Finding
                        issues was only the first step: the client also needed to understand which barriers
                        were most serious and what could realistically be done about them.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        I documented each finding, assessed its severity and explained its likely effect on
                        users. This allowed the report to distinguish issues that could prevent someone from
                        completing a task from those with a more limited, but still important, impact.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Each finding was paired with a recommended solution. Rather than reproducing
                        accessibility criteria without context, I described the interface behaviour that
                        needed to change and suggested a practical direction for resolving it.
                    </p>

                <figure class="case-study-figure case-study-figure--uchicago-report notebook-page">
                    <div class="notebook-page__sheet">
                        <div class="report-excerpt__header" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
                            <p class="report-excerpt__label tag">
                                Example report entry
                            </p>
                        </div>

                        <h3>
                            <p class="report-excerpt__severity">
                                <span class="severity-dots" aria-hidden="true">
                                    <span class="severity-dot severity-dot--high"></span>
                                    <span class="severity-dot severity-dot--medium"></span>
                                    <span class="severity-dot severity-dot--low"></span>
                                </span>
                                <span class="sr-only">Critical issue</span>
                            </p>
                            Associate the facet count with its checkbox
                        </h3>

                        <p class="report-excerpt__location">
                            <strong>Location:</strong> Search filters
                        </p>

                        <div class="report-excerpt__comparison">
                            <section class="existing-markup">
                                <h4>Markup</h4>

                                <pre><code class="language-html">&lt;div class="facet-wrapper"&gt;
    &lt;div class="ui checkbox"&gt;
        &lt;input
            type="checkbox"
            value="open"
            aria-label="Open"
        &gt;
        &lt;label aria-hidden="true"&gt;Open&lt;/label&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;

    &lt;div
        class="facet-count"
        role="note"
        aria-label="13,909 results for Open"
    &gt;
        &lt;span aria-hidden="true"&gt;13,909&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
                            </section>

                            <section class="report-excerpt__analysis">
                                <h4>Analysis</h4>

                                <p>
                                    The <code>role="note"</code> does not represent the purpose of a
                                    numerical results badge. Adding an <code>aria-label</code> to the
                                    separate element also does not associate the count with its
                                    corresponding checkbox.
                                </p>

                                <p>
                                    Although the visual design presents the checkbox and count as one
                                    control, they remain separate elements in the accessibility tree.
                                    The count should instead form part of the checkbox’s accessible
                                    label, so it is announced in context when a user reaches the control.
                                </p>

                                <h4>Recommended implementation</h4>

                                <pre><code class="language-html">&lt;label&gt;
    &lt;input type="checkbox" value="open"&gt;
    Open
    &lt;span class="sr-only"&gt;13,909 results&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/label&gt;

&lt;span
    class="ui circular label facet-count"
    aria-hidden="true"
&gt;
    13,909
&lt;/span&gt;</code></pre>
                            </section>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <figcaption class="board-note" hidden>
                        Each finding combined its location and severity with an explanation of the
                        barrier and an implementation-ready recommendation, giving
                        the client more than a checklist of compliance problems.
                    </figcaption>
                </figure>
            </section>

            <section class="case-study__section">
                <div class="easy-read">
                    <h2>Connecting design and implementation</h2>

                    <p>
                        My front-end experience helped me evaluate the findings from both a user and delivery
                        perspective. I could explain why a pattern created a barrier while also considering how
                        the underlying component might be changed within the constraints of the existing
                        platform.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        This made the report useful to different audiences: the client could understand the
                        significance of each issue, while developers received enough direction to begin
                        discussing implementation rather than first having to interpret an abstract
                        accessibility requirement.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="case-study__section">
                <div class="easy-read">
                    <h2>Outcome and next step</h2>

                    <p>
                        The client accepted the evaluation and its recommendations. They have since asked me
                        to lead a prioritisation workshop in which we will review the findings together and
                        agree which issues should be addressed first.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        This request extends the work beyond a one-off audit. The report now provides a shared
                        evidence base for deciding where changes will have the greatest value and for turning
                        the findings into a realistic programme of improvements.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        This project demonstrates how I approach accessibility as part of product development:
                        identifying barriers, communicating their significance and helping teams move from
                        evaluation towards implementable change.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        <a class="highlight-link client-link"  href="https://knowledge.uchicago.edu/">
                            Visit the Uchicago Knowledge data repository
                        </a>
                    </p>
                </div>
            </section>
        </article>
        <img
                src={bg.src}
                alt=""
                class="background"
        />
    </main>
</BaseLayout>
